(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-antiarrhythmic-class-3-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Class III: Potassium Channel Blockers Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 3","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic can turn the skin blue-gray?","choices":["Dofetilide","Sotalol","Amiodarone","Quinidine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Blue-gray skin color, with photosensitivity, is on amiodarone's toxicity list. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Amiodarone raises the levels of which pair of drugs?","choices":["Codeine and morphine","Verapamil and diltiazem","Lidocaine and esmolol","Digoxin and warfarin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Amiodarone increases digoxin, warfarin, flecainide, phenytoin and procainamide. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A patient starting long-term amiodarone should be told to protect the skin from sunlight because of which effect?","choices":["Photosensitivity","Flushing","Hypersensitivity","Lupus-like rash"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Photosensitivity is on amiodarone's toxicity list, along with a blue-gray skin color. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"How is dofetilide mainly eliminated?","choices":["Unchanged by the kidneys","By hepatic metabolism","By first-pass metabolism","By hepatic acetylation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Dofetilide is mainly eliminated unchanged in the kidneys. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which is a listed indication for sotalol?","choices":["Torsades de pointes with a long QT","Atrial flutter and fibrillation","Heart block","Sick sinus syndrome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sotalol is listed for atrial flutter and fibrillation and for ventricular tachyarrhythmia. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which ventricular arrhythmia is a listed indication for amiodarone?","choices":["Refractory ventricular tachycardia","Ventricular ectopic beats","Sick sinus syndrome","Torsades de pointes with a long QT"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Amiodarone is listed for recurrent ventricular tachycardia or fibrillation resistant to other drugs. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A patient with asthma needs a drug for atrial fibrillation. Which listed drug carries a bronchospasm risk?","choices":["Dofetilide","Amiodarone","Sotalol","Digoxin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sotalol has beta-blocking activity, and bronchospasm is on its toxicity list. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Class III antiarrhythmics prolong repolarization through which ion effect?","choices":["Increased potassium efflux","Reduced potassium efflux","Reduced sodium influx","Reduced calcium influx"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Class III drugs reduce potassium efflux, which prolongs phase 3 repolarization and the refractory period. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Besides the lungs, thyroid, skin and eyes, which organ's dysfunction is listed for amiodarone?","choices":["Kidney","Pancreas","Liver","Bone marrow"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hepatic dysfunction is on amiodarone's toxicity list. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which adverse effect is the main danger of dofetilide?","choices":["Pulmonary fibrosis","Agranulocytosis","Lupus-like syndrome","Torsades de pointes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Dofetilide's listed adverse effects are torsades de pointes and marked QT prolongation. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic is a potent pure potassium channel blocker?","choices":["Amiodarone","Sotalol","Flecainide","Dofetilide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Dofetilide is a potent pure potassium channel blocker, used for normal rhythm in atrial fibrillation. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which eye finding is listed among amiodarone's toxicities?","choices":["Nystagmus","Corneal deposits","Glaucoma","Blurred vision with tinnitus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Corneal deposits are on amiodarone's toxicity list. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 33"}]};
